{"code":0,"msg":"success","data":{"totalNum":9,"list":[{"id":100116,"lang":"en","title":"When Do Larger Batches Help Scale LLM Reinforcement Learning?","desc":"Larger batches expose more parallelism by processing more sequences per update, but under a fixed sample budget, they also leave fewer opportunities to update the model. When does this trade-off reduce wall-clock time-to-target in reinforcement learning? For large language models, answering this question requires accounting for the online generation of training trajectories and the computational asymmetry between generation and training. We develop a framework that connects samples-to-target with end-to-end throughput: a larger batch accelerates training only when its throughput gain exceeds its samples-to-target penalty. This framework also suggests a simple practical strategy—first establish approximate batch-size invariance, then optimize throughput within that regime. Experiments with GRPO and PPO show that learning-rate retuning approximately preserves sample-indexed learning trajectories over a bounded batch range. On fixed hardware, larger batches improve generation throughput by up to 2.29x, and our best measured GRPO configuration reaches the same validation target in 29% less time without additional GPUs.","descTitle":"Abstract","descVisible":1,"author":"RL Team","content":"\n\n\n![Batch scaling overview#100%#auto#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026090811213134_d0d291b9138b4878d3c3ddb2d3c7e34d.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1788837694;2104197694\u0026q-key-time=1788837694;2104197694\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=262651f0542e41c3dfd99a3063f076a01ea47f04)\n\n*!12 Figure 1. Think of training as climbing toward the same capability target. The summit is the shared validation target, each route is a different batch size, and each marker is one optimizer update. After learning-rate retuning, B, 2B, and 4B make similar progress per response, while higher throughput lets the larger batches reach the summit sooner. At 16B, however, the additional sample cost outweighs the throughput gain, so the apparently larger step becomes a slower route. The legend reports normalized time-to-target.!*\n\n# Introduction\n\nPretraining gives large language models a new capability baseline; reinforcement learning determines how far they can continue to advance by learning through exploration and feedback in new environments and tasks. DeepSeek-R1 helped demonstrate how large-scale RL can develop strong reasoning capabilities with little labeled data.[^r1] With Grok 4, xAI pushed the computational scale further: it reported using Colossus, its 200,000-GPU cluster, to run reinforcement learning at pretraining scale, with more than an order of magnitude more compute than in its previous RL work.[^grok] As RL becomes a larger part of model development, its **efficiency** has increasingly large consequences for training budgets.\n\nConsider an illustrative budget: 10,000 GPUs running continuously for 30 days, at an assumed USD 3 per GPU-hour, would cost **USD 21.6 million**. Reaching the same model quality in 10% less time would save **720,000 GPU-hours, or USD 2.16 million** under those assumptions. For a team operating its own cluster, those GPU-hours become capacity for the next experiment.\n\nWhere should we look for that gain? One place is a parameter that every training recipe already has: **batch size**. In practice, batch size enters two recurring decisions:\n\n- **Batch-size tuning: adapting the recipe.** A larger model or a different accelerator type can make the old batch size and learning rate unsuitable. A small batch may underutilize the hardware, while a mismatched learning rate may slow or destabilize learning. The goal is to find a batch size and matching hyperparameters that learn reliably while using the available hardware efficiently.\n- **Batch-size scaling: turning parallelism into faster learning.** If the same model receives more GPUs, can the batch grow with them and reach the target sooner? Even without more GPUs, can a larger generation batch use otherwise idle capacity?\n\nBoth decisions lead to the same practical question: **how do we find the batch size that reaches the target performance in the shortest wall-clock time?**\n\nA larger batch can be either an accelerator or a speed bump. In our experiments, a moderately larger batch with learning-rate retuning preserves learning efficiency while improving throughput. But once the batch becomes too large, the additional sample cost can outweigh the throughput gain. Figure 1 depicts this contrast as different routes to the same summit: a larger batch often helps, but it does not always get the model to the target sooner.\n\n**What scientific principle governs this transition from acceleration to slowdown**, and **can it be turned into a measurable criterion for selecting the batch size that minimizes time-to-target**? To answer this question, we analyze batch scaling under the joint constraints of training dynamics and hardware execution, revisiting LLM RL scaling from first principles. This calls for the co-design of training algorithms and infrastructure. Our[paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.29296)develops the resulting framework.\n\n# From Classical Batch Scaling to LLM Reinforcement Learning\n\n\u003e **How far can we usefully increase the batch?**\n\nThe modern large-batch story first became visible as an engineering breakthrough. In 2017, Goyal et al. trained ResNet-50 on ImageNet in one hour using 256 GPUs and a batch of 8,192, while preserving small-batch accuracy through linear learning-rate scaling and gradual warmup.[^goyal] The result showed that, with the right recipe, a larger batch could turn hardware parallelism into a dramatic reduction in wall-clock time. It also sharpened the next question: \n\n\u003e **how far can this scaling remain effective?**\n\nMcCandlish et al. (2018) connected the point of diminishing returns to the gradient noise scale.[^mccandlish] Shallue et al. (2019) showed that useful data parallelism depends on the workload and that fair batch-size comparisons require hyperparameter tuning.[^shallue] The practical implication is simple: doubling the samples per update does not necessarily halve the updates needed to reach the same target.\n\nA closely related question is whether changing the batch can preserve learning from a fixed number of samples. Hilton, Cobbe, and Schulman (2022) studied this as **batch-size invariance for policy optimization**: after adjusting the relevant hyperparameters, different batch sizes should produce similar learning behavior at equal sample counts.[^invariance] This is both a useful scaling intuition and a fair comparison principle: more samples should generally buy more capability, while the same cumulative sample budget should yield approximately the same capability across well-tuned batch sizes.\n\n\u003e **What changes in LLM RL?**\n\nIn supervised learning, examples already exist in a *fixed* dataset. Increasing the batch mainly changes gradient estimation, optimizer-update frequency, and training parallelism; the model does not have to produce its own training examples.\n\nLLM RL adds an online loop. A Transformer agent first uses the current policy to sample responses or interact with an environment, producing trajectories and rewards. Those newly generated data are then consumed by forward passes, backward passes, and optimizer updates; the updated policy generates the next batch. **The model is therefore both the inference system that produces its training data and the learning system that consumes them.**\n\n![LLM sampling and training share GPU computation#60%#auto#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091516122958_72013eea4c7db8e6a838345f642a499e.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789459950;2104819950\u0026q-key-time=1789459950;2104819950\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=2fd9991431691c535c5753f36aaad19c90463bb0)\n\n*!12 Figure 2. LLM reinforcement learning couples two GPU-intensive processes. Sampling uses autoregressive inference to produce responses and trajectories; training consumes those data to update the model. Batch size shapes both sides: generation batching determines how much inference work can run concurrently, while the training batch determines how much data each optimizer update consumes.!*\n\nBoth processes are GPU-intensive, but they exercise the hardware differently. Sampling generates one token at a time, repeatedly reads model weights, maintains KV caches, and schedules concurrent sequences. Training processes many token positions together but must store activations, compute gradients, and update optimizer states. As we show later, this computational asymmetry causes batch size to affect the two stages differently.\n\n# Two Efficiencies, One Framework\n\nReaching the desired model quality sooner depends on two kinds of efficiency:\n\n- **Sample efficiency** measures how much the model learns per training response. Batch size and its associated hyperparameters determine the progress obtained from a given sample budget.\n- **System efficiency** measures how many training responses the full pipeline processes per second, accounting for generation, model updates, and waiting or synchronization.\n\nLet $J$ denote validation performance, $N$ cumulative training responses, and $t$ elapsed time. Along a smooth approximation to a learning trajectory,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{dJ}{dt}}_{\\text{Learning progress per second}}\n=\n\\underbrace{\\frac{dJ}{dN}}_{\\text{Sample efficiency}}\n\\times\n\\underbrace{\\frac{dN}{dt}}_{\\text{System efficiency}}.\n$$\n\nIf throughput doubles but learning progress per response halves, the two effects cancel. Batch size can change both factors, so we evaluate them jointly. This decomposition provides the conceptual starting point for our fixed-hardware analysis. Actual learning curves are noisy and evaluated at discrete checkpoints, so we use time-to-target for the empirical comparison.\n\nFix a validation target shared by all configurations. Let $N^\\star$ be the number of training responses needed to reach it and $q$ the average end-to-end throughput over that interval. Then\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{N^\\star}{q}.\n$$\n\nRelative to a reference configuration, define the sample-cost ratio $r_N=N^\\star/N_0^\\star$ and throughput ratio $r_q=q/q_0$. Here, $r_N\u003e1$ means the new configuration needs more responses to reach the target (i.e., a sample penalty), while $r_q\u003e1$ means it processes more responses per second (i.e., a throughput gain).\n\n### The criterion: throughput must outweigh sample cost\n\nIt is easy to see that a larger batch finishes sooner exactly when $r_q\u003er_N$.\n\n::: success\nWorked example\nSuppose the reference configuration needs 100,000 responses at 1,000 responses per minute, so it reaches the target in 100 minutes. A larger batch needs 120,000 responses but processes 1,500 responses per minute. Then $r_N=1.2$, $r_q=1.5$, and\n\n$$\n\\frac{T}{T_0}=\\frac{r_N}{r_q} = \\frac{1.2}{1.5}=0.8.\n$$\n\nThe larger batch is 20% faster even though it is less sample-efficient, because the 50% throughput gain exceeds the 20% sample penalty. If throughput improved by only 10%, the ratio would instead be $1.2/1.1\\approx1.09$, making the run about 9% slower.\n:::\n\nThis relationship gives two complementary views:\n\n- **In theory, compare throughput gain with sample penalty.** A larger batch reduces time-to-target only when $r_q\u003er_N$.\n- **In practice, establish invariance before optimizing throughput.** Following the batch-size-invariance principle, retune batch-dependent hyperparameters and identify a range in which learning curves approximately align at equal cumulative response counts. Within that range, $r_N\\approx1$, so higher end-to-end throughput translates more directly into shorter time-to-target. \n\n::: primary\nTakeaway\nBatch size affects both learning per response and end-to-end throughput. A larger batch reduces training time when its throughput gain outweighs any increase in the responses needed to reach the same quality. A simpler practical guideline is to first establish approximate batch-size invariance, then optimize throughput within that range.\n:::\n\n# Step 1: Preserve Learning per Sample\n\nThe first goal is to establish (approximate) batch-size invariance. Its implication is straightforward:\n doubling the batch should roughly halve the optimizer updates needed to reach the same model quality, leaving the total sample requirement approximately unchanged. \n \n After separately retuning batch-dependent hyperparameters, a fair and intuitive scaling comparison should satisfy two expectations: \n- More samples should generally improve performance.\n- At the same cumulative sample count, well-tuned runs with different batch sizes should achieve approximately the same performance.\n\nUnfortunately, this does **not** happen automatically. A larger batch averages gradients over more responses, but it also gives the model fewer opportunities to update within a fixed sample budget. Preserving learning per response therefore requires retuning how far each update moves the policy.\n\n**Retune the learning rate.** For Adam, we use the square-root rule as a starting point:\n\n$$\n\\eta(B)=\\eta_0\\sqrt{\\frac{B}{B_0}}.\n$$\n\nHere, $B_0$ and $\\eta_0$ denote the reference batch size and learning rate. The rule is motivated by prior adaptive-optimizer scaling analysis.[^adam] We test it empirically while holding other optimizer hyperparameters fixed; jointly tuning more batch-dependent hyperparameters may further extend invariance.\n\nTo keep the comparison clean, we evaluate GRPO and PPO using our in-house training infrastructure. Both workloads use one global optimizer update per rollout batch, without minibatch subdivision or rollout reuse, avoiding additional tuning dimensions from minibatch size and optimization epochs.  Because training is stochastic and evaluation is noisy, we expect batch-size invariance to appear as approximate alignment, rather than exact overlap, between empirical learning curves.\n\n### Finding I. Approximate invariance holds over a substantial, bounded range\n\n**GRPO:** These experiments use Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 on a filtered subset of DAPO-MATH-17K, with $G=8$ responses per prompt. We evaluate mean@32 averaged over AIME 2024 and 2025. A training sample is a response retained after filtering and used in an optimizer update; each update contains $P$ retained prompt groups, giving $B_{\\mathrm{train}}=PG$.\n\nWith learning-rate retuning, GRPO maintains approximately aligned sample-indexed learning curves across a 16-fold increase in prompt batch size. The left panel of Figure 3 shows this alignment from $P=64$ to $P=1024$ over the measured training window. At $P=2048$ and $P=4096$, the curves fall below this family at matched sample counts.\n\n![figure2-batch-invariance.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026090823563361_f3819c4dc54707dc7200e0c9fbafecd3.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1788882995;2104242995\u0026q-key-time=1788882995;2104242995\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=5cebf9aa20bc3b3a65c9c80916fe9b5ac4fcd6a8) \n*Figure 3. At fixed group size, retuned GRPO runs align over a bounded range of prompt batches. The largest batches depart from that family, and the decline in actor gradient norm flattens. The gray ±1-percentage-point band is a visual reference, not a confidence interval.*\n\nThe right panel of Figure 3 provides a complementary view of this departure. Across the approximately invariant range, actor gradient norms decrease roughly as $P^{-1/2}$. At $P=2048$ and $P=4096$, this decline becomes shallower, coinciding with the loss of learning-curve alignment in the left panel. This pattern is consistent with diminishing benefits from gradient averaging: beyond this range, adding more responses to each update no longer compensates as effectively for taking fewer updates. \n\n**PPO:** We use an internal Hunyuan policy model and value model, each with 3B active parameters, trained on an internal dataset mixing math, science, and logic tasks. Evaluation performance is averaged across the corresponding benchmarks. Here, each prompt retains one response, so $G=1$ and $B_{\\mathrm{train}}=P$.\n\n![figure5-ppo-invariance.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026090823570254_72679251353a7e9d1653749f891d862b.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1788883024;2104243024\u0026q-key-time=1788883024;2104243024\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=80aea6fb3bf747c81413c0c89adc588c455d5070) \n*Figure 4. Batch scaling in PPO. Left: validation performance versus cumulative training responses. Right: actor and critic gradient norms across batch sizes.*\n\nThe left panel of Figure 4 shows approximately aligned sample-indexed learning curves for batches from 256 through 2048, while batch 4096 falls below this family. This provides complementary evidence that learning-rate retuning can approximately preserve learning per response over a bounded batch range in PPO.\n\nThe right panel reveals a difference between the actor and critic. Actor gradient norms decrease as the batch grows, with the decline becoming shallower at the largest batch. Critic gradient norms, by contrast, remain comparatively flat and noisy. These observations suggest that the actor and critic may have different useful batch scales. Given their distinct training objectives, understanding this difference and whether separate tuning can improve efficiency is an interesting direction for future work.\n\n### Finding II. Total response count as the batch-size unit\n\nIn GRPO, we can enlarge the batch along two axes: sampling more prompts or more responses per prompt. Because responses within a group share the same prompt and contribute to group-relative advantages, these two choices need not reduce variance in the same way. Do they nevertheless produce similar learning behavior when the total response batch is held fixed?\n\nWe compare two pairs of configurations: $(P,G)=(128,8)$ versus $(64,16)$, both with 1,024 responses per update, and $(P,G)=(256,8)$ versus $(128,16)$, both with 2,048 responses per update. The figure denotes group size by $n$, corresponding to $G$ in our notation.\n\nThe left panel shows broadly similar learning trajectories, although the curves do not overlap at every checkpoint. The right panel shows a clearer pairing: configurations with the same total response batch have similar actor gradient norms, while the larger total batch produces consistently smaller norms. Together, these results support using total retained responses, $PG$, as the batch unit for the configurations tested.\n\n![Clipboard_Screenshot_1789012202.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091011534977_8e66a4bd347c404da4e3f880491d113b.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789012429;2104372429\u0026q-key-time=1789012429;2104372429\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=609efae6058ac830cff64182399ad38c63dba9ec)\n*Figure 5. Group-size comparison in GRPO. Left: validation performance versus cumulative training responses. Right: actor gradient norms over the same training window. Solid lines correspond to 1,024 responses per update; dashed lines correspond to 2,048. Blue and orange distinguish group sizes of 8 and 16.*\n\n### Finding III. Keeping the learning rate fixed breaks approximate batch-size invariance\n\nIn practice, adding GPUs often increases both data parallelism and the global batch. If the learning rate and other hyperparameters remain tuned for the smaller batch, training may still look stable—and may even look smoother because gradient noise is lower. But at the same cumulative response count, the larger batch has taken fewer optimizer steps and may not reach the same quality. Stable training therefore does **not** imply batch-size invariance; the larger batch may need substantially more responses to catch up.\n\nWe compare a reference configuration with $P=128$ and $\\eta=10^{-6}$ against two configurations with $P=256$: one keeps the learning rate fixed, while the other increases it to $\\sqrt{2}\\times10^{-6}$ following the square-root rule. Group size is held fixed.\n\n![Clipboard_Screenshot_1789012475.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091011543675_19e6072bf0695bf76cb71eed4cd8bcc4.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789012479;2104372479\u0026q-key-time=1789012479;2104372479\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=cdec201de2913526930a518ceae39f1c44d7306d)\n*Figure 6. Learning-rate control in GRPO. Left: validation performance versus cumulative training responses for the reference batch, a doubled batch with square-root learning-rate scaling, and a doubled batch with the learning rate fixed. Right: optimizer-update counts normalized to the reference first-hit count, illustrating how retuning brings the doubled batch closer to the ideal halving of updates.*\n\nThe left panel shows why retuning matters. With the scaled learning rate, the larger-batch run broadly follows the reference learning curve at matched sample counts. Keeping the learning rate fixed instead produces a substantial lag through much of training. Around 120K responses, for example, the fixed-learning-rate run scores approximately 74%, compared with roughly 77% for the reference and retuned runs.\n\nThe right panel quantifies the difference in optimizer updates. Exact batch-size invariance predicts that doubling the batch should halve the updates needed to reach the same target. The scaled-learning-rate run has a target-crossing interval of **0.50–0.67×** the reference first-hit count, consistent with the ideal **0.50×** at the available checkpoint resolution. The fixed-learning-rate run instead requires **0.75–0.83×**, substantially above that ideal.\n\n::: primary\nTakeaway\nLearning-rate retuning approximately preserves learning per response over a bounded batch range in our GRPO and PPO experiments. In the GRPO group-size comparison, configurations with the same total response batch also show broadly similar learning trajectories and closely matched gradient norms. PPO likewise shows approximate invariance over a bounded range, but the actor and critic exhibit markedly different gradient-norm scaling as the batch grows.\n:::\n\n# Step 2: Improve Throughput Even on Fixed Hardware\n\nOnce learning per sample is approximately preserved, the next question is how to process those samples faster. A familiar approach is to add GPUs and increase data parallelism. But can a larger batch accelerate training without adding hardware? On the same devices, processing more responses per update might seem to simply take proportionally longer. In LLM RL, an opportunity comes from **generation–training computational asymmetry**.\n\n- During autoregressive decoding, each sequence produces one new token at a time. At low concurrency, the GPU repeatedly reads model weights to process relatively few token vectors. More active sequences allow more tokens to share those weight transfers, so the number of responses can grow faster than the time needed to generate them.\n\n- Training has a different execution pattern. Once responses are available, forward and backward passes process many token positions together, already exposing substantial parallelism. In this regime, increasing the batch mainly adds work. \n\nA simple local model summarizes this contrast:\n\n$$\nt_{\\mathrm{gen}}(B_{\\mathrm{gen}})\\approx d_0+d_1B_{\\mathrm{gen}},\n\\qquad\nt_{\\mathrm{train}}(B_{\\mathrm{train}})\\approx\\gamma B_{\\mathrm{train}}.\n$$\n\nHere, $B_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ counts responses in the logical rollout batch, while $B_{\\mathrm{train}}$ counts retained responses consumed by one optimizer update. Our experiments use asynchronous partial rollouts with $B_{\\mathrm{gen}}=2B_{\\mathrm{train}}$, so increasing the training batch also increases the generation batch. The intercept $d_0$ captures generation cost that can be amortized across responses: increasing the batch reduces this cost per response, whereas training time grows approximately in proportion to its batch size. Our[paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.29296)develops this intuition through a roofline view, explaining how batching changes arithmetic intensity and when execution is limited by memory bandwidth or compute. The simplified model above captures the resulting difference between generation and training costs.\n\nThe throughput gain depends on how the execution system translates a larger generation batch into active decoding concurrency, subject to scheduling and memory constraints. We therefore measure end-to-end throughput using retained training responses per second, including time spent on discarded responses, model updates, and pipeline overhead.\n\n### Finding IV. Response-collection time grows sublinearly over the measured range\n\nFigure 7 compares the increase in retained training responses with the increase in collection time, both normalized to the smallest batch. In both workloads, the blue response-count bars grow faster than the orange time bars. Their ratio gives the generation-stage throughput gain.\n\n![figure6-generation-throughput.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026090823591270_60acf35352b316010a5c1b457540fc93.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1788883154;2104243154\u0026q-key-time=1788883154;2104243154\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=c4af122b30ea8a98a786517038ef727d7b8ccdc2) \n*Figure 7. More responses need not take proportionally more collection time. On fixed hardware, a 4× batch increase raises generation-stage throughput by 2.29× in PPO and 1.36× in GRPO.*\n\nIn PPO, increasing the training batch from 256 to 1024 collects four times as many responses per optimizer update, while collection time increases from 39 to 68 seconds. Response-collection throughput therefore improves by $4/(68/39)=2.29\\times$.\n\nIn GRPO, increasing $P$ from 128 to 256 improves generation-stage throughput by 1.31×; increasing it to 512 yields 1.36×. The small additional gain suggests that the available benefit is already flattening over this range.\n\n::: primary\nTakeaway\nGeneration and training scale differently with batch size: generation can amortize model-weight reads across more concurrent sequences, whereas training time grows approximately in proportion to the batch. When decoding has not yet saturated the hardware, doubling the generation batch generally increases collection time by less than 2×, improving generation throughput even without additional GPUs.\n:::\n\n# Step 3: Combine Two Critical Batches to Minimize Time-to-Target\n\nThe preceding results reveal two distinct limits to batch scaling in LLM reinforcement learning:\n- **The critical generation batch** concerns system efficiency. It marks where generation throughput approaches its plateau for a fixed model, decoding engine, response-length distribution, and hardware allocation. Below this scale, increasing the batch can improve hardware utilization; beyond it, further batching yields little additional generation throughput.\n- **The critical training batch** concerns sample efficiency. It marks the upper end of approximate batch-size invariance under learning-rate retuning. Within this range, larger batches need roughly the same number of responses to reach a given target. Beyond it, the benefit of averaging over more responses no longer fully compensates for taking fewer updates, increasing the sample requirement.\n\nThese two limits need not coincide. Figure 8 illustrates their distinct roles, with memory capacity imposing a separate constraint on feasible generation batches. Their interaction determines how far increasing the batch can reduce training time.\n\n![Clipboard_Screenshot_1789019582.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091013530224_dda9df2d9372a59a936f2bf8f8536add.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789019585;2104379585\u0026q-key-time=1789019585;2104379585\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=1d29eeb9d67a3f026e6a5d7bf7a928d94625162f)\n*Figure 8. Two critical batch sizes. Left: generation throughput approaches a plateau. Right: learning per response remains approximately preserved under learning-rate retuning before declining at larger batches. Both curves are schematic.*\n\n### From critical batches to training time\n\nHow do these limits combine to determine training time? Consider a coupled configuration in which generation and training batches increase together. Figure 9 illustrates the case where generation throughput plateaus before learning efficiency deteriorates. In this schematic, the generation plateau also limits further gains in end-to-end throughput, producing a near-optimal range of training batch sizes.\n\n![Coupled batch scaling#600px#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091013191098_eee2e3aee8f67952d5a27f93a44c88b1.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789017553;2104377553\u0026q-key-time=1789017553;2104377553\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=678a100caf9733bf8fab5ebbadc0e33ec2d37a27)\n\u003cdiv style=\"width: 600px; transform: translateX(-50%); margin-left: 50%; display: block;\"\u003e\n*!12 Figure 9. Schematic time-to-target when generation throughput saturates before sample efficiency deteriorates. The star marks the smallest batch on the near-optimal plateau.!*\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\nThis ordering produces three regimes:\n\n- **Learning is preserved and throughput improves.** Increasing the batch reduces training time because the model needs approximately the same number of responses, while the system processes them faster.\n- **Learning is preserved but throughput plateaus.** Further batching offers little additional speed. A range of batch sizes can therefore achieve similar training times.\n- **The sample penalty dominates.** Once larger batches require more responses to reach the target, limited throughput gains can no longer compensate, and training time increases.\n\nThe ordering can also be reversed. If learning efficiency deteriorates before generation throughput plateaus, a larger batch can still finish sooner when its throughput gain outweighs the additional sample cost. \n\n### Empirical Observations\n\nFigure 10 connects this reasoning to our GRPO experiments on fixed hardware, with $G=8$ and $P=128$ as the reference configuration.\n\n![Clipboard_Screenshot_1789017161.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091013124253_737a0bd9bd493c769cf101400160fbe6.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789017165;2104377165\u0026q-key-time=1789017165;2104377165\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=81e009c3fbd643046d3603bd4482937431c679b2)\n*Figure 10. Normalized GRPO training time across prompt batch sizes. Blue points use square-root learning-rate scaling; the orange control keeps the learning rate fixed. All times are normalized to the $P=128$ reference. The star marks the best measured configuration.*\n\nWith learning-rate retuning, increasing $P$ from 128 to 512 and 1024 reduces normalized time-to-target to **0.74× and 0.71×**. At $P=1024$, the sample requirement remains unchanged while throughput improves by 41%, cutting training time from 11.90 to 8.42 hours—**29% less time without adding GPUs**.\n\nLarger batches do not always help. At $P=2048$ and $P=4096$, a **60% increase in required responses** outweighs throughput gains of 30% and 36%, raising training time to **1.23× and 1.18×**.  The deciding factor is whether throughput gains compensate for additional sample cost.\n\n# Conclusion\n\nThe criterion translates into two practical stages:\n\n1. **Align learning per response.** For each candidate training batch, retune the learning rate (and other hyper-parameters), compare learning curves at equal cumulative response counts, and estimate the sample-cost ratio $r_N$ at a shared validation target.\n2. **Measure the systems gain.** Within memory limits, tune generation batching or decoding concurrency, measure end-to-end throughput $q$, and select the feasible configuration that maximizes $q/r_N$. Recheck $r_N$ whenever systems changes alter learning behavior.\n\n![algorithm-two-stage-v2.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026090823593928_f70506f147502883189db553d2849585.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1788883181;2104243181\u0026q-key-time=1788883181;2104243181\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=b4da7c3dcd246703772f094a810d27305bf791bc) \n\n# Future Work\n\nAn interesting question is whether the useful batch range can be predicted, rather than rediscovered through expensive sweeps for every new setting. This requires a mechanistic understanding of why approximate batch-size invariance emerges, what determines its boundary, and why it eventually breaks. Learning-rate retuning is only a first step. A more complete scaling theory should explain how the relevant batch-dependent hyperparameters transfer across models, objectives, task distributions, and training stages.\n\nThe PPO results further suggest that batch size may not have a single universal meaning even within one training algorithm. The actor and critic optimize different objectives and exhibit different gradient-norm scaling behavior; they may therefore have different critical training batches. Understanding these distinct dynamics could motivate separate batch sizes, update schedules, or tuning rules for the two components.\n\nAt the systems level, our observations are specific to autoregressive Transformers, where token-by-token generation and parallel training place different demands on the hardware. Other architectures—including state-space, recurrent, or hybrid models—may shift this generation–training asymmetry and therefore have different critical batch scales. A model- and system-aware RL stack should account for these differences while decoupling generation concurrency from the optimizer batch, without overlooking the resulting trade-offs in queueing, policy staleness, and data freshness.\n\nThe broader goal is therefore not merely to tune batch size, but to develop a predictive, architecture-aware understanding of RL scaling—one that jointly explains training dynamics, hardware execution, and their interaction in determining time-to-target.\n\n# Citation\n\n```bibtex\n@misc{li2026largerbatches,\n  title={When Do Larger Batches Help Scale LLM Reinforcement Learning?},\n  author={Ziniu Li and Jinbo Wang and Guanhua Huang and Feiyuan Zhang and Pengbo Li and Alex Chen},\n  year={2026},\n  eprint={2608.29296},\n  archivePrefix={arXiv},\n  primaryClass={cs.LG},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.29296}\n}\n```\n\n[^goyal]: Goyal et al. (2017), [Accurate, Large Minibatch SGD: Training ImageNet in 1 Hour](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02677).\n\n[^mccandlish]: McCandlish et al. (2018), [An Empirical Model of Large-Batch Training](https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.06162).\n\n[^shallue]: Shallue et al. (2019), [Measuring the Effects of Data Parallelism on Neural Network Training](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.03600). JMLR.\n\n[^invariance]: Hilton, Cobbe, and Schulman (2022), [Batch Size-Invariance for Policy Optimization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.00641). NeurIPS.\n\n[^adam]: Malladi, Lyu, Panigrahi, and Arora (2022), [On the SDEs and Scaling Rules for Adaptive Gradient Algorithms](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10287). NeurIPS.\n\n[^critical]: McCandlish et al. (2018), [An Empirical Model of Large-Batch Training](https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.06162); Shallue et al. (2019), [Measuring the Effects of Data Parallelism on Neural Network Training](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.03600), JMLR.\n\n[^r1]: DeepSeek, [DeepSeek-R1 Release](https://deepseek.com/en/news/deepseek-r1/), January 20, 2025. \n\n[^grok]: xAI, [Grok 4: Scaling Up Reinforcement Learning](https://x.ai/news/grok-4), July 9, 2025. \n\n\n","draftVersionId":"daodup42c3m5m4bg3bm0","publishVersionId":"daodup42c3m5m4bg3bm0","status":2,"publicVisible":1,"publicAt":1790150728,"createdAt":1788944524,"updatedAt":1790150728,"publishedAt":1789707529,"displayPublishTime":1790006400,"renderType":1,"titleVisible":1,"coverImage":"https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/71e4f885/2026091812582093_478a0e952aed78993dfd31c8a980a9b6.jpg?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1789707500;2105067500\u0026q-key-time=1789707500;2105067500\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=70fa1b6c65819356ec8c124350f17432ab2cd95f","coverImageBgColor":"#F5F5F7","customUrl":""},{"id":100100,"lang":"en","title":"Introducing Hy4 preview","descTitle":"Abstract","descVisible":2,"author":"Hy LLM","content":"@@@coverCard\ntype = \"image\"\ndate = \"2026年8月28日\"\nmedia_src = \"https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/9a5ea02f/2026082716220649_1437ddb3b346a01a5e603f2a607d250c.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1787818929;2103178929\u0026q-key-time=1787818929;2103178929\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=87337c8f948a5b795d1bafd4676b33fbba6995fe\"\nimage_alt = \"hy4PreviewBg\"\ntitle = \"Introducing Hy4 preview\"\naspectRatio = \"\"\nmediaWidth = \"100%\"\nmediaMarginTop = \"32\"\nmediaMarginBottom = \"64\"\nvideoLoop = 0\n@@@\n\n@@@space\nheight = 16\n@@@\n\n@@@buttonGroupCard\n\n[[buttons]]\ntext = \"Tencent Cloud\"\nicon = \"tencentcloud\"\nurl = \"https://console.tencentcloud.com/tokenhub/models/detail?modelId=hy4-preview\"\n\n[[buttons]]\ntext = \"GitHub\"\nicon = \"GitHub\"\nurl = \"https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/Hy4-preview\"\n\n[[buttons]]\ntext = \"HuggingFace\"\nicon = \"huggingface\"\nurl = \"https://huggingface.co/tencent/Hy4-preview\"\n\n[[buttons]]\ntext = \"ModelScope\"\nicon = \"modelscope\"\nurl = \" https://modelscope.cn/models/Tencent-Hunyuan/Hy4-preview\"\n\n[[buttons]]\ntext = \"AtomGit\"\nicon = \"https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/9a5ea02f/2026070520372391_5ab131402b8d9602f385640c3cf82490.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1783255045;2098615045\u0026q-key-time=1783255045;2098615045\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=94c5742d352e0f745d2e316a975d5d87ac1c95c0\"\nurl = \" https://gitcode.com/tencent_hunyuan/Hy4-preview\"\n@@@\n\n@@@space\nheight =32\n@@@\nToday we're releasing Hy4 preview, our most capable model to date. It's a 770B-parameter mod…58809 tokens truncated…omposite objective that unifies sequence-level masking ($M_i$) with token-level rectification ($\\Psi$):\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{J}_{\\text{Composite}}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta}) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\substack{(q,a)\\sim \\mathcal{D} \\\\ \\{o_i\\}_{i=1}^G \\sim \\pi_{\\beta}}} \\left[ \\frac{1}{G} \\sum_{i=1}^G M_i(\\rho_i(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{|o_i|} \\sum_{t=1}^{|o_i|} \\Psi(r_{i,t}(\\boldsymbol{\\theta})) \\cdot A_{i,t} \\right) \\right]\n$$\n\nHere, $M_i(\\cdot)$ is the binary sequence mask defined as:\n\n$$\nM_i(\\rho_i) = \\mathbb{I}\\left(C_{\\text{low}}^{(s)} \u003c \\rho_i \u003c C_{\\text{high}}^{(s)} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $C_{\\text{low}}^{(s)}$ and $C_{\\text{high}}^{(s)}$ are the sequence-level trust region bounds (e.g., $[0.995, 1.005]$).\n\nHowever, while SeqClip improves stability by discarding sequences with accumulated drift, it significantly reduces sample efficiency and, crucially, fails to eliminate gradient spikes arising from *consistent* samples, which leads us to Category B.\n\n![performance_first4.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/abbac702/2026020323224636_4118d33f84c5a39b2edc5aea8a54d4f4.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770132167;2085492167\u0026q-key-time=1770132167;2085492167\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=b0b3f2ebb8ca8d8ad53e73ad185b253259969e99)\n*!12 Figure 5: SeqClip postpones training collapse. !*\n\n## Type B (New): Layerwise Gradient Heterogeneity\n\n![Clipboard_Screenshot_1770097891.png#auto#500px#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/abbac702/2026020313513540_50b7b578a470834b738ee5578e0a2b4d.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770097898;2085457898\u0026q-key-time=1770097898;2085457898\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=b472aa1cbf6a9e025955abccc0cfe545000d3100)\n*!12 Figure 6: Layerwise Gradient Heterogeneity: Illustration of gradient norm dynamics across different layers of Qwen3-4B-Instruct. Shallower layers frequently exhibit gradient spikes, whereas deeper layers remain stable throughout the entire training process. !*\n\n![Clipboard_Screenshot_1770174787.png#auto#350px#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/abbac702/2026020411130940_6a8ce2a54933e50e69f4bac99eab3d1a.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770174790;2085534790\u0026q-key-time=1770174790;2085534790\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=ccf69b81ddfe6f65d5bb8a5f890635287fe03d25)\n*!12 Figure 7: Intra-layer Gradient Homogeneity: Illustration of gradient norm dynamics across various sub-modules (Attn, MLP, Norms) within the first layer. In contrast to the disparity across layers, modules within the same layer exhibit highly synchronized spiking patterns. !*\n\n**GradLoc's Discovery**\nThe most critical discovery from GradLoc is **Type B** anomalies. These are sequences that pass all standard safety checks (token IS $\\omega_{i,t} \\approx 1$, sequence IS $\\rho_{i} \\approx 1$), yet they drive the model into collapse.\n\nDetailed inspection uncovers a critical structural dichotomy in these anomalies. First, we observe severe **Layerwise Gradient Heterogeneity**: the gradient explosion is not systemic but strictly localized: where specific layers (typically early in the network) explode with massive norms while subsequent layers remain stable. Conversely, at a finer granularity, we find **Intra-layer Gradient Homogeneity**: sub-modules within the same layer (Attention, MLP, Norms) exhibit highly synchronized spiking patterns. This implies that the instability is a phenomenon driven by collective layer dynamics rather than a single module failure.\n\n**Analysis**\nWe hypothesize that this arises from a compounding effect: minor distribution shifts in early-layer activations (due to data staleness in asynchronous rollouts) could be amplified through the network, or certain attention heads may become transiently unstable on long, out-of-distribution sequences. \n\nUnder layerwise heterogeneity, global gradient clipping becomes misaligned with the failure mode: it suppresses healthy layers to compensate for a few exploding ones. The standard clipping mechanism rescales all gradients uniformly using a single scalar coefficient $\\nu$ based on the global gradient clipping threshold $\\tau$ (e.g., commonly used $\\tau=1$):\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G} \\leftarrow \\mathbf{G} \\cdot \\nu, \\quad \\text{where } \\nu = \\min\\left(1, \\frac{\\tau}{\\|\\mathbf{G}\\|_2}\\right)\n$$\n\nWhen a Type B anomaly occurs:\n1.  The global norm $\\|\\mathbf{G}\\|_2$ is dominated by the few exploding layers.\n2.  The scaling factor $\\nu$ becomes infinitesimally small ($\\nu \\ll 1$) to compensate for the explosion.\n3.  This tiny $\\nu$ is broadcast to all layers. The gradients of well-behaved layers are aggressively suppressed to near zero.\n\nConsequently, the majority of the model optimization momentum is effectively **frozen**, updates remain nearly unchanged in either direction or magnitude. Meanwhile, the optimization trajectory is disproportionately dictated by the erratic direction of the exploding layers. The model appears to be \"stable\" (the global norm is low), but it has ceased learning effectively and is being slowly steered into collapse by the outliers. This necessitates a move from global constraints to layerwise adaptive strategies.\n\n## Addressing Type B: Layerwise Gradient Clipping\n\nIn this section, we aim to address the **Type B anomaly (Layerwise Gradient Heterogeneity)**, which persists even when training-inference consistency is enforced. Inspired by observed \"layerwise gradient heterogeneity\", we propose **layerwise gradient clipping (LayerClip)**. Instead of a fixed global threshold, we enforce a self-adaptive, layerwise constraint on the optimization trajectory of each layer (or FSDP handle).\n\nFor each layer $l$ at step $t$, we maintain a representative statistic $R_l^{(t)}$ of its gradient norm history. We employ an exponential moving average (EMA) or running average (CA) to track the layerwise expected scale[^LayerClip_footnote] :\n\n$$\nR_l^{(t)} =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\beta R_l^{(t-1)} + (1-\\beta) \\|\\nabla_{\\theta_l}^{(t)}\\|_2, \u0026 \\text{(EMA)} \\\\\n(1 - \\frac{1}{t}) R_l^{(t-1)} + \\frac{1}{t} \\|\\nabla_{\\theta_l}^{(t)}\\|_2, \u0026 \\text{(CA)}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nUnlike global static clipping threshold in traditional gradient clipping, our clipping threshold is layerwise adaptive, for layer $l$ is defined dynamically as $\\tau_l = \\alpha \\cdot R_l^{(t)}$, where $\\alpha \u003e 1$ is a hyperparameter. The gradient clipping rule becomes:\n\n$$\n\\tilde{\\nabla}_{\\theta_l}^{(t)} = \\nabla_{\\theta_l}^{(t)} \\cdot \\min\\left(1, \\frac{\\tau_l}{\\|\\nabla_{\\theta_l}^{(t)}\\|_2 + \\epsilon}\\right)\n$$\n\nOur approach combines two key advantages:\n\n1. **Isolation:** An anomaly in Layer $l_A$ triggers clipping only for Layer $l_A$, allowing Layer $l_B$ to update normally. This prevents gradient anomaly layers from dominating the global gradient.\n2. **Adaptivity:** It respects the numerical scale differences between layers, avoiding the bias introduced by a single global threshold.\n\nEmpirically, applying **LayerClip** on the Qwen3-4B-Instruct[^Qwen3] model eliminated the training instability caused by Type B anomalies. By effectively managing the gradient heterogeneity, we observed a more stable training compared to baselines.\n\n![performance.png](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/abbac702/2026020317503771_a4cac944136234128037b420042512e2.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770112238;2085472238\u0026q-key-time=1770112238;2085472238\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=58edc938750dd57f76f5b804106e459c8dc77f03)\n*!12 Figure 8: Training dynamics of different clipping methods. By sequentially addressing three classes of failure cases, TokenClip (TIS, ICEPOP), SeqClip, and LayerClip progressively stabilize training. As each method mitigates a specific anomaly type, training stability improves significantly, leading to consistent gains in performance. !*\n\n# Conclusion\n\nIn this work, we tackle the persistent challenge of training instability in scaling RLVR by shifting the mitigation paradigm from heuristic data filtering to infrastructure-level diagnosis. Through a principled anomaly localization tool, **GradLoc**, we achieved precise localization of gradient spikes, thereby revealing a critical limitation in existing methods. We demonstrated that while IS constraints effectively address training-inference inconsistency, they fail to mitigate a newly identified class of \"consistent\" anomalies manifest as severe **Layerwise Gradient Heterogeneity**. To resolve this, we introduced Layerwise Gradient Clipping (**LayerClip**), which clips gradient based on local layer statistics rather than a global norm. Empirical validation confirms that effectively managing this heterogeneity significantly extends training stability. Our study establishes a systematic framework for understanding and analyzing RLVR training, and accelerates large-model training strategies in both academic and industrial scenarios.\n\n# Future Work\n\nRLVR faces a significant disconnect between engineering and theory. Due to the high complexity of engineering implementation, researchers often struggle to systematically observe training dynamics. Consequently, many promising theoretical explorations are hindered by cumbersome engineering debugging. This has historically shifted the community's focus on optimization theory and optimizer research heavily toward pre-training. To bridge this gap, we plan to open-source and iterate on **GradLoc**, transforming gradient anomaly localization into a routine diagnostic as accessible as monitoring loss curves. By lowering the barrier to fine-grained observation, we aim to empower the community to look inside the engineering \"black box\" and advance the theoretical foundations of RLVR optimization.\n\nFurthermore, while **LayerClip** effectively mitigates instability by containing layerwise heterogeneity, it does not eliminate the root cause. This heterogeneity likely signals deeper, under-explored physical and statistical mechanisms in large model training. Moving forward, it is important to investigate these fundamental principles to transcend heuristic clipping and design optimization algorithms that are mathematically robust from first principles.\n\n\u003e **Contributors**: Guanhua Huang$^*$, Tingqiang Xu$^*$, Jinbo Wang$^*$, Guangming Sheng, Siheng Li, Evander Yang, Kejiao Li, Yunxiang Li, Zenan Xu, Qi Yi, Kyrierl Deng, Ziyuan Nan, Yuhao Jiang, Chenchen Zhang, Taiqiang Wu, Feiyuan Zhang, Junhao Wang, Bo Zhou$^{\\dagger}$, Alex Chen$^{\\dagger}$, Di Wang, Shunyu Yao.\n\u003e  ($*$: equal contribution; ${\\dagger}$: corresponding author.)\n\n# Citation\n```\n@misc{huang-xu-wang-2026-gradloc,\n  title = {Stabilizing {RLVR} via Token-level Gradient Diagnosis and Layerwise Clipping},\n  author = {Huang, Guanhua and Xu, Tingqiang and Wang, Jinbo and Sheng, Guangming and Li, Siheng and Yang, Evander and Li, Kejiao and Li, Yunxiang and Xu, Zenan and Yi, Qi and Deng, Kyrierl and Nan, Ziyuan and Jiang, Yuhao and Zhang, Chenchen and Wu, Taiqiang and Zhang, Feiyuan and Wang, Junhao and Zhou, Bo and Chen, Alex and Wang, Di and Yao, Shunyu},\n  year = {2026},\n  url = {https://hy.tencent.com/research/100015}\n}\n```\n\n\n# Acknowledgments\nWe thank our Pre-train Team colleagues for their valuable discussions on gradient instability and for providing feedback on the deployment of GradLoc in pre-training scenarios. We also appreciate the fruitful discussions with our colleagues from the Post-train Team and thank the Infrastructure and Platform Teams for their technical support. Finally, we thank Weinan E, Zhouwang Yang, Lei Wu, and Mingze Wang for their in-depth exchanges regarding the optimization dynamics of Large Language Models.\n\n# Appendix\n## Proof for Theorem 1\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe proof relies on the concentration properties of Chi-squared random variables and a union bound over the steps of the algorithm. Without loss of generality, one can set $s=1$.\n\n**1. Preliminaries and Notation.**  \nThe algorithm proceeds in $L = \\log_2 N$ steps. At step $j \\in \\{1, \\dots, L\\}$, let $\\mathcal{S}_\\text{anom}$ denote the subset containing the anomalous vector $g_{k^*}$, and $\\mathcal{S}_\\text{noise}$ denote the opposing subset containing only standard Gaussian noise vectors. Let $m_j = N / 2^j$ be the number of vectors in each subset at step $j$.\n\nDefine the sum vectors for the two subsets as:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{v \\in \\mathcal{S}_\\text{noise}} v, \\quad Y = \\sum_{v \\in \\mathcal{S}_\\text{anom}} v.\n$$\n\nBased on the additivity of independent Gaussians, the distributions are:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, m_j I_D), \\quad Y \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, (m_j - 1 + \\sigma^2) I_D).\n$$\n\nConsequently, their squared norms scale with Chi-squared distributions:\n\n$$\n\\|X\\|^2 \\sim m_j \\chi^2_D, \\quad \\|Y\\|^2 \\sim (m_j + \\Delta) \\chi^2_D,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta = \\sigma^2 - 1$ is the excess variance contribution from the anomaly.\n\n**2. Concentration Bounds.**  \nWe utilize the Laurent-Massart bounds for a Chi-squared variable $Z \\sim \\chi^2_D$. For any $t \u003e 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbb{P}(Z - D \\geq 2\\sqrt{Dt} + 2t) \\leq e^{-t}, \\\\\n\\mathbb{P}(Z - D \\leq -2\\sqrt{Dt}) \\leq e^{-t}.\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nLet $\\delta_j$ be the failure probability allowed at step $j$. We set $t = \\log(1/\\delta_j)$.\n\nThe algorithm makes an error at step $j$ if $\\|X\\|^2 \\geq \\|Y\\|^2$. We seek to bound the probability of this event. Using the concentration inequalities, with probability at least $1 - 2\\delta_j$, the norms are bounded by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\|X\\|^2 \\leq m_j (D + 2\\sqrt{D t} + 2t) := B_{noise}^{upper}, \\\\\n\\|Y\\|^2 \\geq (m_j + \\Delta) (D - 2\\sqrt{D t}) := B_{anom}^{lower}.\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nA sufficient condition for success ($\\|Y\\| \u003e \\|X\\|$) is $B_{anom}^{lower} \u003e B_{noise}^{upper}$. Substituting the terms:\n\n$$\n(m_j + \\Delta)(D - 2\\sqrt{Dt}) \u003e m_j (D + 2\\sqrt{Dt} + 2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\Delta = \\sigma^2 - 1$, a sufficient condition can be:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 - 1 \\geq 5 \\frac{m_j}{\\sqrt{D}} \\sqrt{t} \\text{ and } t \\le \\frac{D}{144}.\n$$\n\n**3. Union Bound.**  \nThe algorithm succeeds only if it makes the correct decision at all $L = \\log_2 N$ steps. Let $E_j$ be the event of failure at step $j$. We assign an equal failure budget to each step, setting $\\delta_j = (\\frac{\\epsilon}{1+\\epsilon})^j := \\mu(\\epsilon)^j$.\n\nBy the union bound, the total probability of failure is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{P}(\\text{Failure}) \\leq \\sum_{j=1}^{L} \\mathbb{P}(E_j) \\leq \\sum_{j=1}^{L} \\mu(\\epsilon)^j \\leq \\frac{\\mu(\\epsilon)}{1 - \\mu(\\epsilon)} = \\epsilon.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t = \\log(1/\\mu(\\epsilon)^j)$ into our condition derived in Step 2, we obtain the requirement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\u0026 t \\le \\frac{D}{144} \\\\\n\\Leftrightarrow\n\u0026 -j\\log \\mu(\\epsilon) \\leq \\frac{D}{144} \\text{ for } 1 \\leq j \\leq \\log_2 N \\\\\n\\Leftarrow\n\u0026 -\\log_2 N \\log\\frac{\\epsilon}{2} \\le \\frac{D}{144} \\text{ by } \\frac{\\epsilon}{1+\\epsilon} \\geq \\frac{\\epsilon}{2} \\\\\n\\Leftrightarrow\n\u0026 \\epsilon \\geq 2e^{-\\frac{D}{144\\log_2 N}}.\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\u0026 \\sigma^2 - 1 \\geq 5 \\frac{m_j}{\\sqrt{D}} \\sqrt{-j\\log \\frac{\\epsilon}{1+\\epsilon}} \\text{ for } 1 \\leq j \\leq \\log_2 N \\\\\n\\Leftrightarrow \u0026\n\\sigma^2 - 1 \\geq 5 \\frac{N}{\\sqrt{D}} \\sqrt{\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{\\epsilon})}\\frac{\\sqrt{j}}{2^j} \\text{ for } 1 \\leq j \\leq \\log_2 N\\\\\n\\Leftrightarrow \u0026\n\\sigma^2 - 1 \\geq 2.5 \\frac{N}{\\sqrt{D}} \\sqrt{\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{\\epsilon})}.\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nThe second $\\Leftrightarrow$ is by $\\frac{\\sqrt{j}}{2^j} \\leq \\frac{1}{2}$, it suffices to meet that requirement as stated in Theorem 1. Under this condition, the correct subset is selected at every step, leading to the unique anomaly $g_{k^*}$ with probability at least $1-\\epsilon$.\n\n[^OpenAI o1]: [OpenAI o1 System Card](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.16720)\n\n[^DeepSeek-R1]: [DeepSeek-R1 incentivizes reasoning in LLMs through reinforcement learning](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09422-z)\n\n[^verl]:[HybridFlow: A Flexible and Efficient RLHF Framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.19256)\n\n[^areal]:[AReaL: A Large-Scale Asynchronous Reinforcement Learning System for Language Reasoning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24298)\n\n[^slime]:[slime: An LLM post-training framework for RL Scaling](https://github.com/THUDM/slime)\n\n[^KVQuant]: [KVQuant: Towards 10 Million Context Length LLM Inference with KV Cache Quantization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.18079)\n\n[^vLLM]: [Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3600006.3613165)\n\n[^Kimi k1.5]: [Kimi k1.5: Scaling Reinforcement Learning with LLMs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12599)\n\n[^LR_Scheduling]: [Beyond Precision: Why Training-Inference Mismatch is an Optimization Problem and How Simple LR Schedules Fix It](https://richardli.xyz/mismatch-lr-schedule)\n\n[^TIS]: [Your Efficient RL Framework Secretly Brings You Off-Policy RL Training](https://fengyao.notion.site/off-policy-rl)\n\n[^CISPO]: [MiniMax-M1: Scaling Test-Time Compute Efficiently with Lightning Attention](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13585)\n\n[^GPPO]: [CE-GPPO: Coordinating Entropy via Gradient-Preserving Clipping Policy Optimization in Reinforcement Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.20712)\n\n[^IcePop]: [Small Leak Can Sink a Great Ship—Boost RL Training on MoE with IcePop!](https://ringtech.notion.site/icepop)\n\n[^SeqClipNoDetail]: [When Speed Kills Stability: Demystifying RL Collapse from the Training-Inference Mismatch](https://yingru.notion.site/When-Speed-Kills-Stability-Demystifying-RL-Collapse-from-the-Training-Inference-Mismatch-271211a558b7808d8b12d403fd15edda)\n\n[^SeqClip]: [DeepSeek-V3.2: Pushing the Frontier of Open Large Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02556)\n\n[^FSDP]: [PyTorch FSDP: Experiences on Scaling Fully Sharded Data Parallel](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.11277)\n\n[^GRPO]: [DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300)\n\n[^Qwen3]: [Qwen3 Technical Report](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.09388)\n\n[^FP16]:[Defeating the Training-Inference Mismatch via FP16](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.26788v1)\n\n[^1st_approx]: [Stabilizing Reinforcement Learning with LLMs: Formulation and Practices](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.01374)\n\n[^exp_setting_footnote]: Experiment Settings: Model: Qwen3-4B-Instruct; learning rate $\\eta=2 \\times 10^{-6}$; train prompt batch size = 128; rollout number = 8; maximum response length = 32,768; partial rollout chunk size = 4,096; TIS clipping range is [0, 5]; ICEPOP clipping range is [0.5, 5.0]; SeqClip clipping range is [0.995, 1.005].\n\n[^FSDP_footnote]: In FSDP, gradients accumulate across micro-batches. If we performed rank search first, evaluating each rank subset would require re-accumulating gradients over all $M$ micro-batches ($M$ forward-backward passes). By isolating the target micro-batch first, the subsequent rank search only requires re-computation for a single micro-batch, reducing complexity from $O(M \\cdot \\log W)$ to $O(M + \\log W)$.\n\n[^Phase4_footnote]: In practice, this operates as a Depth-First Search (DFS) on a binary segmentation tree rather than a standard single-path search. Identifying the primary \"culprit\" is sufficient to deduce systemic issues without exhaustive enumeration. With a budget slightly above the theoretical lower bound ($\\approx \\log N$), GradLoc leverages DFS to backtrack and pinpoint co-occurring anomalous tokens with negligible cost, balancing detection speed with diagnostic completeness.\n\n[^threshold_footnote]: For clarity, a simplified derivation is presented here; the actual FSDP implementation accounts for additional coefficients introduced by sharding strategies, as detailed in our code.\n\n[^LayerClip_footnote]: Our key insight suggests other potential solutions. Specifically, the gradient of each layer can be explicitly regularized by coupling it to smoothed cross-layer statistics, such as CA or EMA, providing a possible alternative to address layerwise gradient heterogeneity.\n\n[^omega_footnote]: Note that tokens with small $\\omega_{i,t}(\\theta)$ are safe only after the loss function is corrected to use $\\omega_{i,t}(\\theta)$. Before this correction (in standard GRPO), they are not safe because the coefficient of $\\|\\nabla \\log \\pi_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\\|$ is $r_{i,t}(\\theta) \\approx 1$ (since values deviating from 1 are clipped and do not participate in the loss), which fails to suppress the gradient norm.","draftVersionId":"d67ds4c2c3m0lbl55ud0","publishVersionId":"d67ds4c2c3m0lbl55ud0","status":2,"publicVisible":1,"publicAt":1770971763,"createdUid":"kyrohuang","createdAt":1768936042,"updatedAt":1770971763,"publishedAt":1770971763,"displayPublishTime":1770971763,"renderType":1,"titleVisible":1,"coverImage":"https://hy-apikey-manager-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/image/apikey/1782458322345774148_fb89002f86c684ec6b10b23ee8459eb2.png","coverImageBgColor":"#F9F8F4","customUrl":""},{"id":100025,"lang":"en","title":"Learning from context is harder than we thought","descTitle":"Abstract","descVisible":1,"author":"Shihan Dou and Pluto Zhou","content":"\nCL-bench project page：[www.clbench.com](https://www.clbench.com)\nCL-bench paper link: [download CL-bench paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03587)\nCL-bench code repository：[Tencent-Hunyuan/CL-bench](https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/CL-bench)\nCL-bench data repository：[tencent/CL-bench](https://huggingface.co/datasets/tencent/CL-bench)\n\n## We need AI to be context learners\n\nOver the past few years, language models have become astonishingly capable. Frontier systems can now solve International Mathematical Olympiad problems, navigate complex coding challenges, and pass professional exams that take humans years to prepare for. They excel at taking tests, spinning out long chains of reasoning to win at benchmarks. \n\nBut as impressive as these feats are, they obscure a simple truth: being a \"test-taker\" is not what most people need from an AI.\n \nLook at our own daily work. A developer skims documentation for a tool they've never seen and immediately starts debugging. A player picks up a rulebook for a new game and learns by playing. A scientist sifts through complex experimental logs to derive a new theorem from fresh data. \n\nIn all these cases, humans aren't relying solely on a fixed body of knowledge learned years ago. **We are learning, in real-time, from the context right in front of us.**\n\n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020317325150_35d45a7b64f8fdc190e182f83a22d697.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770111173;2085471173\u0026q-key-time=1770111173;2085471173\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=5991c589114c85f95eed08b68a0e4315d7b90590) !12 Three examples from real-world work and life scenarios. **Case1**: converting natural-language flight requests into safe, compliant SDK pseudocode given the SkyNet drone SDK documentation (~70K words); **Case2**: learning a new game by reading its full rulebook (~15K words) and analyzing a hidden-room scenario with possible outcomes; and **Case 3**: analyzing 300 raw experiment logs to derive the underlying relation and estimate the resonance constant. !\n\n \nCurrent language models do not handle context this way. They rely primarily on parametric knowledge—information compressed into their weights during massive pre-training runs. At inference time, they function largely by recalling this static, internal memory, rather than actively learning from new information provided in the moment.\n \n**This creates a structural mismatch.** We have optimized models to excel at reasoning over what they already know yet users need them to solve tasks that depend on messy, constantly evolving context. We built models that rely on what they know from the past, but we need context learners that rely on what they can absorb from the environment in the moment. \n \nTo bridge this gap, we must fundamentally change our optimization direction.\n \n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318070766_e21cd6ca6ab3526f93f7217f786c075e.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113228;2085473228\u0026q-key-time=1770113228;2085473228\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=97115eb2505576770c92bd755d77c0337a87bf00) !12 Paradigm shift in language models. !\n \n## Measuring context learning with CL-bench\n\nTo evaluate how far current models are from being true context learners, we built [**CL-bench**](https://www.clbench.com),  a benchmark that tests whether LMs can learn new knowledge from context and apply it correctly.\n \nCL-bench contains 500 complex contexts, 1,899 tasks, and 31,607 verification rubrics, all crafted by experienced domain experts. It tests a simple but demanding requirement: solving each task must require learning something from context that is not already in the model's pre-training.\n \nCL-bench spans four broad types of real-world context learning scenarios:\n\n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318095374_e3e242482eec4aced0b1edfafe80d5cf.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113395;2085473395\u0026q-key-time=1770113395;2085473395\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=81f2628c3378802403399aefb589913dea42c1a8) !12 Context taxonomy of CL-bench. !\n1. **Domain knowledge reasoning**: The context provides specific domain knowledge (such as a fictional legal system, innovative financial tools, or niche expertise). The model is required to use this knowledge to reason and solve concrete problems.\n2. **Rule system application**: The context provides a newly defined formal system (such as a new game mechanism, mathematical formalism, programming syntax, or technical standards). The model must understand and apply these rules to execute tasks.\n3. **Procedural task execution**: The context provides complex process systems (such as workflows, product manuals, and operational guides). The model must understand and apply this procedural information to complete tasks.\n4. **Empirical discovery \u0026 simulation**: The context provides experimental data, observational records, or simulated environments within a complex system. Unlike the previous categories, which involve deductive reasoning, this category is focused on inductive reasoning and is the most challenging. The model must discover potential patterns or laws from the data and apply them to solve tasks.\n\n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318131932_cc41023e55acac12fb2bc3bb05e6bd7b.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113600;2085473600\u0026q-key-time=1770113600;2085473600\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=1de418e634d9e605db8b9de6b379ea5276380b37) !12 Examples from CL-bench. Solving these tasks requires LMs to learn from the provided context. These four cases span: **(1)** Adjudicating a real dispute under a 23k-word newly enacted statute; **(2)** Given a new programming language specification, implement a time-based control program with a fixed termination condition; **(3)** Executing code in an unseen programming framework, and **(4)** Given the technical specifications and long-term environmental policy scenarios, simulate the sustainable global supply of a critical technology metal. More cases are available at [**huggingface: CL-bench**](https://huggingface.co/datasets/tencent/CL-bench). ! \n \nTogether, these categories capture both deductive and inductive forms of context learning that arise naturally in real-world work. \n\nMore details on CL-bench can be found in our paper [**CL-bench**](https://www.clbench.com).\n\n### Design principles and features of CL-bench\n\nCL-bench is built around a simple but strict design principle: **every task must require learning new knowledge from context**. Each context in CL-bench is fully self-contained. All information needed to solve its tasks is provided explicitly within the context itself—no external retrieval is required, and no hidden assumptions are allowed.\n \n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318125274_921211381e9aa29678a154ef53726718.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113574;2085473574\u0026q-key-time=1770113574;2085473574\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=96a7897fb319e3cb83c43604be1067cb0cd6fff1) !12 In CL-bench, solving tasks requires models to learn new knowledge from the corresponding context. !\n \nTo ensure that performance genuinely reflects context learning, rather than memorization or data leakage, CL-bench adopts a contamination-free design:\n\n1. **Fictional Creation**: Experts create entirely fictional content, such as designing a complete legal system for a fictional country, including novel case precedents and legal principles, or creating a new programming language with unique syntax and semantics.\n2. **Modification of Existing Content**: Experts modify real-world content to create variations, such as altering historical events, changing scientific and mathematical definitions, or modifying technical documents and standards.\n3. **Integration of Niche and Emerging Content**: Experts incorporate niche or recently emerging content that is largely underrepresented in pre-training datasets, such as cutting-edge research discoveries, newly released product manuals, or technical documents, as well as domain-specific knowledge from specialized fields.\n\nWithout any context provided, the state-of-the-art model, GPT-5.1 (High), is only able to solve **less than 1%** of tasks. This starkly demonstrates that the data is contamination-free, as the model is almost entirely incapable of solving the tasks without learning from the context.\n \nMoreover, CL-bench is designed to be high in complexity and sequence-dependent. **51.1%** of tasks require sequential dependencies, meaning the solution to a later task depends on the outcome of earlier interactions. This multi-turn design significantly increases the difficulty of the tasks. On average, domain experts spend about **20 hours** annotating each context to ensure the quality and depth of the task construction.\n \nEach task in CL-bench is fully verifiable. On average, each context is associated with **63.2** verification rubrics, and each task contains **16.6** evaluation criteria. The correctness of every task is evaluated from multiple angles, ensuring comprehensive assessment.\n \n### Empirical findings\n\nWe evaluate ten state-of-the-art LMs on CL-bench, and results show that **context learning remains a significant challenge**, and some insights into it.\n\n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318123788_661e1c0763df519f913d6adf9cfcd0c9.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113558;2085473558\u0026q-key-time=1770113558;2085473558\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=46bfcedf0b81eb695589013f06992f80c9f76c72) !12 Task solving rate of ten frontier LLMs on the CL-bench. All LMs are evaluated in reasoning mode, with results reported as mean ± std (%) across three runs. !\n \nOn average, models solve only **17.2%** of tasks. Even the best-performing model, GPT-5.1 (High), achieves just **23.7%**. \n\nIn other words, despite having access to all the information required to solve each task, frontier models fail on the vast majority of them. This indicates that current SOTA models largely fail at context learning.\n \n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318121894_8d92f402029a90784567b7da33df723e.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113540;2085473540\u0026q-key-time=1770113540;2085473540\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=f2ba105b9ba02ac0336b4457c16f569b9f8fb605) !12 Distribution of error types across models. !\n\nSeveral additional patterns stand out:\n\n- **Ignoring or misusing context is the dominant failure mode.** Many errors do not stem from missing information, but from models overlooking critical details in the context or applying them incorrectly. In many cases, models revert to pre-trained assumptions even when the context explicitly defines new rules, concepts, or procedures.\n- **Long-context reasoning and instruction following are necessary, but not sufficient.** Case analyses show that models struggle to track dependencies across long contexts or to precisely follow novel constraints tend to perform worse. However, even models that handle long inputs well and follow instructions reliably still fail many tasks. Context learning requires more than long-context reasoning and instruct-following abilities.\n- **Inductive reasoning from empirical data exhibits greater difficulty than deductive application.** Deductive tasks let models apply rules and procedures explicitly given in context, while empirical discovery \u0026 simulation tasks require **inductive reasoning**—inferring laws and patterns from data or exploring virtual environments. Models perform significantly worse on these tasks, often under 10% success in observational and simulation cases, with high variance across runs, showing that discovering laws and patterns is much harder than applying rules.\n\n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020317365744_16c047a51afcb5b054e210dc054ec8d0.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770111418;2085471418\u0026q-key-time=1770111418;2085471418\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=e5c6d2869b9d04a337449bb1923d09a0eb387920)!12 Performance comparison of GPT-5.1 under high versus low reasoning effort settings across all subcategories. !\n\n- **Higher reasoning effort generally improves context learning.** Increasing **reasoning effort** improves performance for some models, allowing deeper engagement with complex context. For example, GPT-5.1 gains ~6% on management and experimental tasks. Other models show little or negative effect, indicating that reasoning alone isn’t sufficient—models must also correctly absorb and organize context information.\n\n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020317380911_759274f14975c805d5147ed464f200cd.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770111490;2085471490\u0026q-key-time=1770111490;2085471490\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=74ff706314ecde87b0b9c4d412d32ddfe1415a96)!12 Performance across different input length ranges, with similar trends observed as context changes. !\n\n- **Context learning difficulty correlates with context length, but short context can also be complex.** Longer contexts generally make tasks harder for all models, confirming long-context processing as a key bottleneck. Yet even short contexts can be complex when they contain dense information, subtle rules and dependencies, or strict constraints, meaning difficulty comes from both **length and complexity** of contextual information.\n\nMore findings can be found in our paper [**CL-bench**](https://www.clbench.com). \n\nTaken together, CL-bench makes one conclusion unavoidable: today's frontier LMs are still far from reliable context learners.\n\nThis helps explain a common frustration in real-world deployments. Even with increasingly sophisticated context engineering, models often fail in subtle but consequential ways. Providing context is not enough if the model cannot truly learn from it.\n\nContext learning as a foundational capability has been largely overlooked. Until it improves, LMs will remain brittle precisely in the settings where we most want them to help: messy, dynamic, real-world environments.\n \n## The Future\n\nIf context learning improves significantly, the role of humans in AI systems would shift. Rather than primarily providing training data, humans would focus on context engineering, designing and supplying the richest and most relevant context for each task. Humans' role would transition from **training data providers** toward **context providers.**\n \nBut there's a catch. Even then, context learning is ephemeral: models adapt within a window and forget once it clears. The deeper question is: how can we make knowledge from context persistent? This knowledge goes beyond facts—it includes skills, experience, and patterns that help models generalize across tasks.\n \n![image#100%#center](https://hy-model-ap-prod-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/llm-blog/default/68558e8d/2026020318120045_085a836f1c810951051075b316fef168.png?q-sign-algorithm=sha1\u0026q-ak=AKIDRl074nOsGdJ9zjMsCRWP3ShmgS3VtX4S\u0026q-sign-time=1770113522;2085473522\u0026q-key-time=1770113522;2085473522\u0026q-header-list=\u0026q-url-param-list=\u0026q-signature=1a176956bc508293697fd5ba0d0c19169f43107c) !12 Memory is the key to consolidating the experience that language models acquire through context learning. !\n\n\nFor now, the focus is clear. it's time to make context learning a reality!\n \n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nI would like to thank **Pluto Zhou**. The concept of context learning originated from his proposal in 2024. \n\nI would also like to express my sincere gratitude to **Shunyu Yao** for his encouragement and invaluable feedback. His repeated and insightful suggestions and revisions have greatly helped us refine this work. I also want to thank **Shichun Liu** and **Junjie Shan** for their help with the writing and presentation of this blog.\n\nFinally, I'm deeply grateful to my advisors, **Tao Gui**, **Qi Zhang**, and **Xuanjing Huang**, for their unwavering support of my research.\n \n \n*Written on January, 2026, as the new year begins.*","draftVersionId":"d61dtt42c3mer51kjig0","publishVersionId":"d61dtt42c3mer51kjig0","status":2,"publicVisible":1,"publicAt":1770112927,"createdUid":"pierredou","createdAt":1770090168,"updatedAt":1770090898,"publishedAt":1770090898,"displayPublishTime":1770090898,"renderType":1,"titleVisible":1,"coverImage":"https://hy-apikey-manager-1258344703.cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com/image/apikey/1782458318247956406_bb4e3dc150217983a4e78ed44d182106.png","coverImageBgColor":"#DDE3EF","customUrl":""}]}}
